{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"language":"python","display_name":"Python 3","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.6.6","mimetype":"text/x-python","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","nbconvert_exporter":"python","file_extension":".py"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"none","dataSources":[],"isInternetEnabled":true,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":false}},"nbformat_minor":4,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"source":"<a href=\"https://www.kaggle.com/code/rizviabiha/langgraph-coreconcepts?scriptVersionId=370230290\" target=\"_blank\"><img align=\"left\" alt=\"Kaggle\" title=\"Open in Kaggle\" src=\"https://kaggle.com/static/images/open-in-kaggle.svg\"></a>","metadata":{},"cell_type":"markdown"},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"\n**Langgraph is an orchestration framework for building intelligent stateful and multi step LLM workflows.it enables advanced features like parallelism,loops,branching,memory and resumability-making it ideal for agentic and production grade AI applications.it models your logic as a graph of nodes (tasks) and edges(routing) instead of a limear chain.**\n\n\n\n\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# LLM Workflows\n\n## 1. What Are LLM Workflows?\n\nLLM workflows are **step-by-step processes using which we can build complex LLM applications.**\n\nEach step in a workflow performs a distinct task, such as:\n\n- Prompting\n- Reasoning\n- Tool calling\n- Memory access\n- Decision-making\n\nWorkflows can have different execution patterns:\n\n- Linear\n- Parallel\n- Branched\n- Looped\n\nThese patterns allow us to build complex behaviours such as:\n\n- Retries\n- Multi-agent communication\n- Tool-augmented reasoning\n\n## 2. Common Types of LLM Workflows\n\nThere are five common workflow patterns:\n\n1. Prompt Chaining\n2. Routing\n3. Parallelization\n4. Orchestrator-Workers\n5. Evaluator-Optimizer\n\n---\n\n# 1. Prompt Chaining\n\n### Definition\n\nPrompt Chaining is a workflow in which **the output of one LLM call becomes the input of the next LLM call.**\n\nWe divide a complex task into smaller, sequential steps. Each step performs a specific task, and its output is passed to the next step.\n\n### How does it work?\n\nSuppose we want to generate a detailed report on a particular topic.\n\nInstead of asking an LLM to generate the entire report in one go, we divide the task into smaller steps.\n\n**Step 1: First LLM Call**\n\n- Input: Topic\n- Task: Generate an outline for the given topic.\n- Output: An outline.\n\n**Step 2: Second LLM Call**\n\n- Input: The outline generated by the first LLM call.\n- Task: Check whether the outline is valid and complete.\n- Output: A decision (Pass or Fail).\n\n**Step 3: Gate (Decision Point)**\n\nThe gate checks the output of the previous LLM call.\n\n- If the output passes the check, the workflow moves forward to LLM Call 2.\n- If the output fails the check, the workflow exits.\n\n**Step 4: Third LLM Call (LLM Call 2)**\n\n- Input: The approved outline.\n- Task: Generate a detailed report based on the outline.\n- Output: A detailed report.\n\n**Step 5: Fourth LLM Call (LLM Call 3)**\n\n- Input: The report generated in the previous step.\n- Task: Refine or improve the report.\n- Output: Final report.\n\nThe final output is returned.\n\n### Example\n\nSuppose we want to generate a detailed report of around 5000 words on a particular topic.\n\nWe can divide the task into:\n\n```text\nTopic\n  |\n  v\nLLM Call 1: Generate Outline\n  |\n  v\nGate: Check the Outline\n  |\n  | Pass\n  v\nLLM Call 2: Generate Detailed Report\n  |\n  v\nLLM Call 3: Refine the Report\n  |\n  v\nFinal Output\n```\n\nIf the outline fails the validation, the workflow exits instead of proceeding.\n\n**Important:** Prompt chaining is useful when a complex task can be divided into smaller tasks where each step depends on the previous step's output.\n\n---\n\n# 2. Routing\n\n### Definition\n\nRouting is a workflow in which **an input is analysed and directed to one of several possible paths based on its content or requirements.**\n\nA router decides which LLM, tool, or processing path should handle the given input.\n\n### How does it work?\n\n1. We receive an input.\n2. The input is passed to an LLM Router.\n3. The router analyses the input and decides which path is suitable.\n4. The input is sent to the selected LLM.\n5. The selected LLM processes the input.\n6. The result is returned as the final output.\n\n### Example 1: Customer Support Chatbot\n\nSuppose we are building a customer support chatbot.\n\nUsers can ask different types of questions:\n\n- Technical questions\n- Billing-related questions\n- General queries\n\nInstead of sending every query to the same LLM, we can use a router.\n\n```text\n                 User Query\n                     |\n                     v\n                LLM Router\n                     |\n          ___________|___________\n         |           |           |\n         v           v           v\n      LLM 1        LLM 2       LLM 3\n    Technical     Billing      General\n         |           |           |\n         |___________|___________|\n                     |\n                     v\n                  Output\n```\n\nFor example:\n\n- If the user asks about a technical issue, the router sends the query to LLM 1.\n- If the user asks about a payment problem, the router sends it to LLM 2.\n- If the user asks a general question, the router sends it to LLM 3.\n\n### Example 2: Report Generation\n\nSuppose a user gives a topic and asks for a particular type of report.\n\nThe router can decide which LLM or processing path should handle the request based on its requirements.\n\nFor example:\n\n- A scientific topic can be directed to a scientific report workflow.\n- A general topic can be directed to a general report workflow.\n\n**Important:** Routing is useful when we have multiple possible processing paths and need to choose the most appropriate one based on the input.\n\n---\n\n# 3. Parallelization\n\n### Definition\n\nParallelization is a workflow in which **multiple tasks are executed simultaneously instead of executing them one after another.**\n\nA complex task is divided into smaller subtasks, and multiple LLM calls process those subtasks at the same time.\n\nTheir outputs are then collected and combined by an Aggregator.\n\n### How does it work?\n\n1. We receive an input.\n2. The input is divided into multiple subtasks.\n3. Different LLM calls execute these subtasks simultaneously.\n4. Each LLM produces its own output.\n5. An Aggregator collects all the outputs.\n6. The Aggregator combines them into a final output.\n\n### Example: Generating a Report\n\nSuppose we want to generate a detailed report about a particular topic.\n\nInstead of asking one LLM to research everything, we can divide the task into three subtasks.\n\n- LLM Call 1: Research the history of the topic.\n- LLM Call 2: Research its current situation.\n- LLM Call 3: Research its future possibilities.\n\nAll three LLM calls execute simultaneously.\n\nTheir outputs are then passed to an Aggregator, which combines the information into a single report.\n\n```text\n                  Input\n                    |\n          __________|__________\n         |          |          |\n         v          v          v\n      LLM Call 1  LLM Call 2  LLM Call 3\n         |          |          |\n         |__________|__________|\n                    |\n                    v\n                Aggregator\n                    |\n                    v\n               Final Output\n```\n\n### Example: Breaking a Task into Subtasks\n\nSuppose the task is to analyse a company.\n\nWe can divide it into:\n\n- LLM 1: Analyse the company's financial performance.\n- LLM 2: Analyse its competitors.\n- LLM 3: Analyse customer reviews.\n\nAll three tasks can be performed simultaneously, and their results can be combined by the Aggregator.\n\n**Important:** Parallelization is useful when multiple independent subtasks can be executed simultaneously.\n\nIt can reduce execution time compared to executing all tasks sequentially.\n\n---\n\n# 4. Orchestrator-Workers\n\n### Definition\n\nOrchestrator-Workers is a workflow in which **an Orchestrator divides a complex task into smaller subtasks, assigns those subtasks to different workers, and then combines their results using a Synthesizer.**\n\nHere, the Orchestrator decides how to divide the task, while the Workers execute the assigned subtasks.\n\n### How does it work?\n\n**Step 1: Input**\n\nWe receive a complex task.\n\n**Step 2: Orchestrator**\n\nThe Orchestrator analyses the task and decides:\n\n- How many subtasks are required?\n- What are the individual subtasks?\n- Which worker should handle each subtask?\n\n**Step 3: Workers**\n\nDifferent workers execute their assigned subtasks.\n\nIn this workflow, workers are represented by separate LLM calls.\n\nThey can execute their tasks simultaneously.\n\n**Step 4: Synthesizer**\n\nOnce all the workers complete their tasks, their outputs are passed to a Synthesizer.\n\nThe Synthesizer combines all the outputs into one final result.\n\n**Step 5: Output**\n\nThe final output is returned.\n\n### Example: Generating a Research Report\n\nSuppose we ask an AI system:\n\n\"Generate a detailed research report on Artificial Intelligence.\"\n\nThis is a complex task, so the Orchestrator divides it into smaller subtasks.\n\n**Orchestrator:**\n\nIt decides to divide the research into three parts:\n\n- Research the history of AI.\n- Research the current applications of AI.\n- Research the future of AI.\n\n**Workers:**\n\n- Worker 1 researches the history of AI.\n- Worker 2 researches the current applications of AI.\n- Worker 3 researches the future of AI.\n\nAll workers execute their tasks simultaneously.\n\n**Synthesizer:**\n\nThe Synthesizer collects the outputs from all three workers and combines them into one detailed research report.\n\n```text\n                    Input\n                      |\n                      v\n                 Orchestrator\n                      |\n             _________|_________\n            |         |         |\n            v         v         v\n         Worker 1  Worker 2  Worker 3\n            |         |         |\n            |_________|_________|\n                      |\n                      v\n                  Synthesizer\n                      |\n                      v\n                  Final Output\n```\n\n### Difference Between Parallelization and Orchestrator-Workers\n\nBoth workflows execute multiple tasks in parallel, but there is an important difference.\n\n**Parallelization:**\n- The tasks are generally predefined.\n- Multiple LLM calls execute those tasks simultaneously.\n- Their outputs are combined using an Aggregator.\n\n**Orchestrator-Workers:**\n- The Orchestrator dynamically analyses the main task.\n- It decides how to divide the task into subtasks.\n- It assigns the subtasks to workers.\n- A Synthesizer combines the results.\n\n**Important:** Orchestrator-Workers is useful when the number or nature of subtasks may depend on the input, rather than being completely predefined.\n\n---\n\n# 5. Evaluator-Optimizer\n\n### Definition\n\nEvaluator-Optimizer is a workflow in which **one LLM generates a solution, while another LLM evaluates that solution and provides feedback.**\n\nIf the solution is rejected, the feedback is sent back to the Generator so that it can improve the solution.\n\nThis process continues until the solution is accepted.\n\n### How does it work?\n\n**Step 1: Input**\n\nWe receive a task or query.\n\n**Step 2: LLM Call Generator**\n\nThe Generator creates an initial solution based on the given input.\n\n**Step 3: LLM Call Evaluator**\n\nThe Evaluator checks the generated solution.\n\nIt evaluates whether the solution meets the required criteria.\n\n**Step 4: Decision**\n\nThere are two possible outcomes:\n\n- **Accepted:** If the solution meets the required criteria, it is returned as the final output.\n- **Rejected:** If the solution does not meet the criteria, the Evaluator provides feedback.\n\n**Step 5: Feedback Loop**\n\nThe feedback is sent back to the Generator.\n\nThe Generator uses this feedback to improve the previous solution and generates a new version.\n\nThe new solution is again sent to the Evaluator.\n\nThis process continues until the solution is accepted.\n\n### Example: Generating a Scientific Report\n\nSuppose we want to generate a scientific research report.\n\n**LLM Call Generator:**\n- Receives the research topic.\n- Generates a scientific report.\n\n**LLM Call Evaluator:**\n- Checks the report.\n- Evaluates its accuracy, completeness, and quality.\n- Decides whether the report is acceptable.\n\nIf the report is rejected, the Evaluator provides feedback, such as:\n\n- Add more scientific details.\n- Improve the explanation.\n- Correct inaccurate information.\n- Include missing sections.\n\nThe Generator uses this feedback to improve the report.\n\nOnce the Evaluator accepts the solution, the final report is returned.\n\n```text\n                   Input\n                     |\n                     v\n             LLM Call Generator\n                     |\n                     v\n             LLM Call Evaluator\n                     |\n              _____Decision_____\n             |                  |\n             v                  v\n          Accepted           Rejected\n             |                  |\n             v                  v\n        Final Output       Feedback\n                                |\n                                |\n                                v\n                         LLM Generator\n                                |\n                                v\n                         Improved Solution\n                                |\n                                v\n                         LLM Evaluator\n```\n\n**Important:** Evaluator-Optimizer is useful when the quality of the output matters and we want to improve the solution through repeated evaluation and feedback.\n\n---\n\n# 3. Quick Revision: All Five Workflows\n\n| Workflow | Main Idea | Important Component |\n|---|---|---|\n| Prompt Chaining | Output of one step becomes input of the next | Sequential LLM calls |\n| Routing | Select the appropriate path based on input | Router |\n| Parallelization | Execute multiple independent tasks simultaneously | Aggregator |\n| Orchestrator-Workers | Divide a complex task into subtasks and assign workers | Orchestrator and Synthesizer |\n| Evaluator-Optimizer | Generate, evaluate, and improve a solution | Generator and Evaluator |\n\n---\n\n# 4. Final Understanding\n\nLLM workflows help us divide complex AI applications into smaller, manageable steps.\n\nDifferent workflow patterns solve different problems:\n\n1. **Prompt Chaining:** When one task depends on the output of another task.\n2. **Routing:** When we need to choose the appropriate processing path.\n3. **Parallelization:** When multiple independent tasks can run simultaneously.\n4. **Orchestrator-Workers:** When a complex task needs to be dynamically divided into subtasks.\n5. **Evaluator-Optimizer:** When we need to evaluate and improve the generated output through feedback.\n\nThese workflow patterns help us build more structured and reliable LLM applications.\n\nThey are also important building blocks for understanding how to design complex workflows using LangGraph.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# UPSC Essay Practice Platform Using LangGraph\n\n## 1. Problem Statement\n\nWe want to build a website that acts as an essay practice platform for UPSC aspirants.\n\nStudents visit our website to practise writing essays for the UPSC Mains examination.\n\nThe platform follows this process:\n\n1. Whenever a student visits the website, the system generates an essay topic.\n2. The student writes an essay on the given topic.\n3. The student submits the essay.\n4. The system analyses the essay based on different evaluation criteria.\n5. Based on the evaluation, the system generates a final score.\n6. If the score clears the cutoff, the system congratulates the student.\n7. If the score does not clear the cutoff, the system provides feedback highlighting areas that need improvement.\n8. The student gets an option to rewrite the essay and submit it again.\n\n## 2. How Can We Implement This Using LangGraph?\n\nBefore implementing this application using LangGraph, we need to design a proper workflow.\n\nA workflow defines:\n- What tasks need to be performed.\n- In which order the tasks should execute.\n- Which tasks can execute simultaneously.\n- Where decisions need to be made.\n- Whether a task needs to be repeated.\n\nOnce we have designed the workflow, we can represent it as a graph using LangGraph.\n\nThe graph will contain different nodes connected through edges.\n\n## 3. Essay Evaluation Criteria\n\nAfter the student submits an essay, our system evaluates it using three different criteria.\n\nEach evaluation task focuses on a separate aspect of the essay.\n\n### A. Clarity of Thought\n\nEvaluates how clearly the student communicates their ideas.\n\nIt checks:\n- Whether the ideas are understandable.\n- Whether the essay has logical flow.\n- Whether the arguments are presented clearly.\n\n**Example:** If a student writes an essay about women's empowerment, the evaluator checks whether the student presents their thoughts in a clear and understandable manner.\n\n**Score:** Out of 5.\n\n### B. Depth of Analysis\n\nEvaluates the depth of the student's analysis.\n\nIt checks:\n- Strength of arguments.\n- Critical thinking.\n- Depth of understanding.\n- Whether the student explores the topic properly.\n\n**Example:** If the topic is climate change, the evaluator checks whether the student discusses its causes, consequences and possible solutions rather than just giving a basic introduction.\n\n**Score:** Out of 5.\n\n### C. Language\n\nEvaluates the language quality of the essay.\n\nIt checks:\n- Grammar.\n- Vocabulary.\n- Fluency.\n- Tone.\n\n**Example:** If a student makes frequent grammatical mistakes or uses inappropriate vocabulary, the language evaluator identifies these issues.\n\n**Score:** Out of 5.\n\n## 4. Complete Workflow\n\nThe complete workflow consists of the following steps:\n\n### Step 1: GenerateTopic\n\nThe system generates a relevant UPSC-style essay topic and presents it to the student.\n\n**Example:**\n\nTopic: *The Role of Artificial Intelligence in India's Development.*\n\n### Step 2: WriteEssay\n\nThe student writes an essay on the generated topic and submits it through the website.\n\nThe submitted essay becomes the input for the evaluation process.\n\n### Step 3: EvaluateEssay (Parallel Evaluation)\n\nAfter receiving the essay, the system sends it to three different evaluation tasks.\n\nThese three tasks run **in parallel**, because they evaluate different aspects of the same essay and do not depend on one another.\n\n- EvaluateClarity – Evaluates clarity of thought.\n- EvaluateDepth – Evaluates depth of analysis.\n- EvaluateLanguage – Evaluates language quality.\n\nEach evaluator generates its own score and feedback.\n\nFor example:\n\n| Evaluation | Score |\n|---|---:|\n| Clarity of Thought | 4/5 |\n| Depth of Analysis | 3/5 |\n| Language | 4/5 |\n| **Total** | **11/15** |\n\n### Step 4: AggregateResults\n\nAfter all three evaluation tasks finish, their results are collected.\n\nThe system combines the individual scores and calculates the final score.\n\nExample:\n\nClarity + Depth + Language\n\n= 4 + 3 + 4\n\n= **11/15**\n\n### Step 5: ConditionalRouting\n\nNow the system compares the final score against the predefined cutoff.\n\nThere are two possible outcomes:\n\n**Case 1: Score meets the cutoff**\n\nIf the student's score meets or exceeds the cutoff, the workflow moves to `ShowSuccess`.\n\n**Case 2: Score is below the cutoff**\n\nIf the student's score is below the cutoff, the workflow moves to `GiveFeedback`.\n\n### Step 6: GiveFeedback\n\nThe system provides targeted suggestions to help the student improve the weak areas of their essay.\n\nFor example:\n\n- Improve the depth of your analysis.\n- Include stronger arguments.\n- Work on grammatical mistakes.\n- Improve the clarity of your ideas.\n\n### Step 7: CollectRevision (Optional Loop)\n\nAfter receiving feedback, the student gets an option to rewrite the essay.\n\nThere are two possibilities:\n\n- **Yes:** The student revises and resubmits the essay. The workflow loops back to `WriteEssay` and the essay is evaluated again.\n- **No:** The student decides not to attempt another revision, and the workflow ends.\n\n### Step 8: ShowSuccess\n\nIf the student clears the cutoff, the system congratulates the student and ends the workflow.\n\nExample:\n\n\"Congratulations! You have successfully cleared the essay evaluation cutoff. Keep practising!\"\n\n## 5. Complete Graph Flow\n\nThe workflow can be represented as follows:\n\n```text\n              START\n                |\n                v\n          GenerateTopic\n                |\n                v\n            WriteEssay\n                |\n                v\n        EvaluateEssay\n                |\n       +--------+--------+\n       |        |        |\n       v        v        v\n   Evaluate  Evaluate  Evaluate\n   Clarity   Depth     Language\n       |        |        |\n       +--------+--------+\n                |\n                v\n         AggregateResults\n                |\n                v\n       ConditionalRouting\n                |\n          +-----+-----+\n          |           |\n      Score >=      Score <\n       Cutoff        Cutoff\n          |           |\n          v           v\n      ShowSuccess  GiveFeedback\n          |           |\n          v           v\n         END      CollectRevision\n                      |\n                 +----+----+\n                 |         |\n                Yes        No\n                 |         |\n                 v         v\n             WriteEssay   END\n                 |\n                 v\n          EvaluateEssay\n                 |\n                ...\n```\n\n## 6. Example: Complete Execution\n\nSuppose a student visits our UPSC essay practice website.\n\n**Generated topic:**\n\n\"The Role of Artificial Intelligence in India's Development.\"\n\nThe student writes and submits an essay.\n\nThe system evaluates the essay:\n\n| Evaluator | Score |\n|---|---:|\n| Clarity of Thought | 3/5 |\n| Depth of Analysis | 2/5 |\n| Language | 4/5 |\n| **Final Score** | **9/15** |\n\nSuppose the predefined cutoff is 10/15.\n\nSince the student's score is 9/15, which is below the cutoff, the system routes the workflow to `GiveFeedback`.\n\n**Feedback generated:**\n\n1. Your language and grammar are good.\n2. Your clarity of thought needs some improvement.\n3. Your depth of analysis is weak.\n4. Include more detailed arguments and examples to support your points.\n\nThe student decides to rewrite the essay.\n\nThe workflow loops back to `WriteEssay`.\n\nAfter resubmission, the system evaluates the revised essay again.\n\nNew scores:\n\n| Evaluator | Score |\n|---|---:|\n| Clarity of Thought | 4/5 |\n| Depth of Analysis | 4/5 |\n| Language | 4/5 |\n| **Final Score** | **12/15** |\n\nSince 12/15 is greater than the cutoff of 10/15, the workflow moves to `ShowSuccess`.\n\nThe student receives a congratulatory message, and the workflow ends.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Important Things to Understand\n\n1. **Sequential execution:** Some tasks execute one after another. For example, generating a topic happens before writing the essay.\n\n2. **Parallel execution:** The three evaluation tasks (Clarity, Depth and Language) execute simultaneously because they are independent tasks.\n\n3. **Aggregation:** The results from all three evaluators are collected and combined to calculate the final score.\n\n4. **Conditional routing:** The next step depends on whether the student's score clears the cutoff.\n\n5. **Loop:** If the student fails to clear the cutoff but wants to improve, the workflow allows them to rewrite and resubmit the essay.\n\n6. **Termination:** The workflow ends either when the student clears the cutoff or when the student chooses not to revise the essay.\n\n**Final understanding:** We first understand the complete application requirement, divide it into smaller tasks, decide their execution order and dependencies, and then represent the entire process as a graph using LangGraph.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# State and Reducers in LangGraph\n\n# 1. State in LangGraph\n\n## What is State?\n\nIn LangGraph, **State is the shared memory that flows through the workflow. It holds all the data being passed between nodes as the graph runs.**\n\nEvery node can:\n- Read the data available in the state.\n- Perform its assigned operation.\n- Return new data or updated values to the state.\n- Pass the updated state to the next node.\n\nThink of state as a Python dictionary containing key-value pairs.\n\n## Example 1: Adding Two Numbers and Multiplying the Result by 2\n\nSuppose we want to build a simple workflow that:\n\n1. Takes two numbers as input.\n2. Adds those numbers.\n3. Multiplies the sum by 2.\n4. Prints the final result.\n\nWe can implement this workflow using LangGraph.\n\n### Step 1: Define the State\n\nFirst, we need to decide what information our workflow needs to maintain.\n\nWe will define three key-value pairs:\n\n```python\n{\n    \"first_number\": 10,\n    \"second_number\": 20,\n    \"result\": 0\n}\n```\n\nHere:\n\n- `first_number` stores the first input number.\n- `second_number` stores the second input number.\n- `result` stores the result of our operations.\n\n### Step 2: Perform Addition\n\nThe first node takes `first_number` and `second_number` from the state and performs addition.\n\n```python\nresult = first_number + second_number\n```\n\nFor example:\n\n```python\n10 + 20 = 30\n```\n\nNow, the node updates the `result` key in the state.\n\nUpdated state:\n\n```python\n{\n    \"first_number\": 10,\n    \"second_number\": 20,\n    \"result\": 30\n}\n```\n\n### Step 3: Perform Multiplication\n\nThe updated state is passed to the next node.\n\nThis node takes the existing `result` and multiplies it by 2.\n\n```python\nresult = result * 2\n```\n\nTherefore:\n\n```python\n30 * 2 = 60\n```\n\nNow the `result` key gets updated again.\n\nUpdated state:\n\n```python\n{\n    \"first_number\": 10,\n    \"second_number\": 20,\n    \"result\": 60\n}\n```\n\n### Step 4: Print the Final Result\n\nThe final node reads the result from the state and prints it.\n\n**Final Output:**\n\n```text\nFinal Result: 60\n```\n\n### Complete Workflow\n\n```text\n       INPUT\n          |\n          v\n  first_number = 10\n  second_number = 20\n  result = 0\n          |\n          v\n    Addition Node\n          |\n          v\n     10 + 20 = 30\n          |\n          v\n    Update State\n     result = 30\n          |\n          v\n  Multiplication Node\n          |\n          v\n       30 * 2\n          |\n          v\n    Update State\n     result = 60\n          |\n          v\n     Print Result\n          |\n          v\n         END\n```\n\n### Important Observation\n\nIn this example, we are updating the same `result` key multiple times.\n\nFirst, it stores 30, and later it stores 60.\n\nThis works perfectly fine because we only need the latest result.\n\n**By default, when a node updates an existing state key, its previous value gets replaced by the new value.**\n\nHowever, this default behaviour can create problems in certain scenarios.\n\n---\n\n# 2. When Default State Updates Become a Problem\n\nLet's understand another scenario.\n\nSuppose we are building a chatbot using LangGraph.\n\nThe chatbot consists of:\n- One Human (User).\n- One LLM (Chatbot).\n\nBoth communicate with each other in a loop.\n\nWe maintain their conversation in the state using a key called `messages`.\n\n### Example Conversation\n\nSuppose the user starts a conversation:\n\n**Human:**\nHi, my name is Abiha.\n\n**LLM:**\nIt is good to know you, Abiha!\n\nNow, suppose the state stores messages using the following structure:\n\n```python\n{\n    \"messages\": \"Hi, my name is Abiha\"\n}\n```\n\nAfter the LLM responds, the state gets updated:\n\n```python\n{\n    \"messages\": \"It is good to know you, Abiha!\"\n}\n```\n\nNotice what happened!\n\nThe previous message:\n\n```text\nHi, my name is Abiha\n```\n\nhas been replaced by:\n\n```text\nIt is good to know you, Abiha!\n```\n\nNow, suppose the user asks:\n\n**Human:**\nWhat is my name?\n\nThe chatbot might not know the answer because the previous message containing the user's name was overwritten.\n\nThe conversation history was not preserved.\n\n### Why Did This Happen?\n\nBecause the default state update behaviour replaces the existing value with the new value.\n\nIn this scenario, replacing the previous message is not what we want.\n\nWe want to preserve all the messages exchanged between the human and the LLM.\n\nThis is where **Reducers** come into the picture.\n\n---\n\n# 3. Reducers in LangGraph\n\n## What are Reducers?\n\n**Reducers in LangGraph define how updates from nodes are applied to the shared state.**\n\nEach key in the state can have its own reducer, which determines whether new data:\n\n1. Replaces the existing value.\n2. Merges with the existing value.\n3. Gets added to the existing value.\n\nIn simple words, reducers control how state updates happen.\n\n### Default Behaviour vs Reducer Behaviour\n\n| Default update | Reducer update |\n|---|---|\n| New value replaces old value. | New value is processed according to the reducer. |\n| Previous value can be lost. | Previous values can be preserved or combined. |\n| Useful when only the latest value matters. | Useful when we need to accumulate information. |\n\n## Example 2: Using a Reducer in Our Chatbot\n\nInstead of storing a single message as a string, we will maintain a list of messages.\n\nInitially:\n\n```python\n{\n    \"messages\": []\n}\n```\n\nNow, we want every new message to be added to the existing list instead of replacing it.\n\nFor this, we can use the `add` reducer.\n\n### Step 1: Define State with a Reducer\n\n```python\nfrom typing import TypedDict, Annotated\nfrom operator import add\n\nclass ChatState(TypedDict):\n    messages: Annotated[list[str], add]\n```\n\nLet's understand this:\n\n- `messages` is the key in our state.\n- `list[str]` means it stores a list of strings.\n- `Annotated` allows us to attach additional information to the type.\n- `add` specifies the reducer that will combine the old and new values.\n\nHere, `operator.add` combines two lists by concatenating them.\n\n### Step 2: First Human Message\n\nThe user says:\n\n```text\nHi, my name is Abiha.\n```\n\nThe state becomes:\n\n```python\n{\n    \"messages\": [\n        \"Hi, my name is Abiha.\"\n    ]\n}\n```\n\n### Step 3: LLM Response\n\nThe LLM responds:\n\n```text\nIt is good to know you, Abiha!\n```\n\nInstead of replacing the existing message, the reducer appends the new message to the existing list.\n\nUpdated state:\n\n```python\n{\n    \"messages\": [\n        \"Hi, my name is Abiha.\",\n        \"It is good to know you, Abiha!\"\n    ]\n}\n```\n\n### Step 4: User Asks Another Question\n\nThe user asks:\n\n```text\nWhat is my name?\n```\n\nThe new message is added to the existing list.\n\nUpdated state:\n\n```python\n{\n    \"messages\": [\n        \"Hi, my name is Abiha.\",\n        \"It is good to know you, Abiha!\",\n        \"What is my name?\"\n    ]\n}\n```\n\nNow the LLM can access the earlier conversation and identify the user's name.\n\n### Step 5: LLM Responds\n\n```text\nYour name is Abiha!\n```\n\nThe reducer appends this response too.\n\nFinal state:\n\n```python\n{\n    \"messages\": [\n        \"Hi, my name is Abiha.\",\n        \"It is good to know you, Abiha!\",\n        \"What is my name?\",\n        \"Your name is Abiha!\"\n    ]\n}\n```\n\nOur chatbot can now retain the conversation history within the graph state.\n\n---\n\n# 4. Difference Between State and Reducers\n\n| State | Reducers |\n|---|---|\n| Stores the data used by the workflow. | Defines how updates are applied to that data. |\n| Contains key-value pairs. | Controls how values are replaced, merged or accumulated. |\n| Shared between nodes. | Determines how nodes update shared state. |\n| Example: `messages`, `result`, `topic`. | Example: `add` reducer. |\n\n## Final Understanding\n\n- **State:** Where we store the data that flows between nodes.\n- **Default Update:** Replaces the existing value with the new value for a particular key.\n- **Reducer:** Controls how new values are combined with existing values.\n- **Why Reducers?** Sometimes replacing old values is not desirable. For example, in a chatbot, we need to preserve the entire conversation history rather than just the latest message.\n\n**Remember:** In LangGraph, we use state to maintain information across nodes and reducers to control how that information changes as the workflow executes.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# LangGraph Execution Model\n\nThe **LangGraph Execution Model** describes how a graph is defined, compiled, and executed. It explains how information flows between nodes, how nodes become active, how updates are applied to the state, and when graph execution stops.\n\n## 1. Graph Definition\n\nBefore executing a graph, we need to define its structure.\n\nWe define three main things:\n\n1. **State Schema**\n   - Defines what information the graph will store.\n   - Specifies the key-value pairs that will be available in the shared state.\n\n2. **Nodes**\n   - Nodes are functions that perform specific tasks.\n   - Each node receives the current state, performs an operation, and returns an update.\n\n3. **Edges**\n   - Edges define which node connects to which other node.\n   - They determine the flow of execution between nodes.\n\n**Example:**\n\nIn our UPSC Essay Evaluation System, we might define:\n\n- State: essay text, topic, scores, feedback and evaluation round.\n- Nodes: GenerateTopic, WriteEssay, EvaluateLanguage, EvaluateDepth and FinalEvaluation.\n- Edges: Connections between these nodes that determine the execution flow.\n\n## 2. Compilation\n\nAfter defining the graph, we compile it.\n\nWe use:\n\n`graph.compile()`\n\n- Compilation checks the graph structure.\n- It prepares the graph for execution.\n- It ensures that the graph is ready to run.\n\nCompilation does not execute the graph. It prepares the graph so that we can execute it.\n\n## 3. Invocation\n\nOnce the graph is compiled, we can execute it using:\n\n`graph.invoke(initial_state)`\n\n- We provide the initial state as input.\n- LangGraph sends the initial state as a message to the entry node(s).\n- The graph starts executing according to the defined connections.\n\n**Example:**\n\nFor our essay evaluation system, the initial state might contain the essay topic or other required input.\n\nThe first active node receives this information and starts performing its task.\n\n## 4. Super-Steps Begin\n\nLangGraph execution proceeds in rounds called **Super-Steps**.\n\nA super-step represents one round of graph execution.\n\nDuring each super-step:\n\n- All active nodes (nodes that have received messages) execute in parallel.\n- Each active node performs its assigned task.\n- Each node returns an update (message) to the shared state.\n\n**Important:** Nodes that become active because of messages received during the current super-step execute in the next super-step.\n\n### Example\n\nConsider our UPSC Essay Evaluation System.\n\nAfter the student submits an essay, three evaluation nodes are activated:\n\n1. EvaluateClarity – evaluates clarity of thought.\n2. EvaluateDepth – evaluates depth of analysis.\n3. EvaluateLanguage – evaluates language quality.\n\nAll three nodes can execute in parallel during the same super-step because their evaluations are independent.\n\nAfter completing their tasks, they return their respective scores to the state.\n\n## 5. Message Passing and Node Activation\n\nNodes communicate with downstream nodes through messages passed along edges.\n\nThe process works as follows:\n\n1. An active node performs its task.\n2. It returns an update.\n3. The update is applied to the shared state.\n4. Messages are passed to downstream nodes through edges.\n5. Nodes receiving messages become active for the next super-step.\n\n**Example:**\n\nIn our essay evaluation system:\n\n- EvaluateClarity returns a clarity score.\n- EvaluateDepth returns a depth score.\n- EvaluateLanguage returns a language score.\n\nThese updates are passed to the FinalEvaluation node.\n\nOnce FinalEvaluation receives the required information, it becomes active in the next super-step and calculates the final score.\n\n## 6. Halting Condition\n\nGraph execution stops when both of the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n1. No nodes are active.\n2. No messages are in transit.\n\nThis means there is no remaining work to perform and no further node is waiting to receive a message.\n\n**Example:**\n\nIn our UPSC Essay Evaluation System:\n\n- If the student clears the cutoff, the Success node congratulates the student and the graph ends.\n- If the student does not clear the cutoff, the Fail/Feedback node provides feedback.\n- If the student chooses to retake the essay, the graph follows the loop back to the Write Essay node.\n- If the student does not want to retake it, the graph ends.\n\nTherefore, the graph continues executing as long as there is work to perform and stops when the halting condition is reached.\n\n---\n\n## Complete Execution Flow\n\nThe overall LangGraph execution process can be summarized as:\n\n1. **Graph Definition** – Define state, nodes and edges.\n2. **Compilation** – Compile the graph using `.compile()`.\n3. **Invocation** – Start execution using `.invoke(initial_state)`.\n4. **Super-Steps** – Active nodes execute in parallel and return updates.\n5. **Message Passing** – Updates and messages travel through edges, activating downstream nodes.\n6. **Halting Condition** – Execution stops when no nodes are active and no messages are in transit.\n\n**Remember:** Graph Definition → Compilation → Invocation → Super-Steps → Message Passing → Halting Condition.","metadata":{}}]}